$DIR/main.p:52:2: r.n holds a view of l (l.head, line 50), which was moved to the task started at line 51, so r.n can no longer be used here
    r.n, part of r, looks at memory that l owns, without owning it: the view was
    made by l.head at line 50. A view of an owner held in a local variable may
    be used only while the variable keeps the value the view was made from. At
    line 51 l was moved to the task started, so the view may look at memory that
    was released, or at a value that now belongs to something else.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use r.n before line 51, or move l only after the last use of r.n
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
